<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="android:editTextBackground">@drawable/rn_edit_text_material</item>
        <!--
          The ground under the app itself, for the window rather than for a
          React view.

          Without this the window is AppCompat's default — which is dark under
          DayNight on a device in dark mode — and it shows in two places React
          cannot paint: behind the activity during a configuration change, and
          in the gap between the launch screen being torn down and the first JS
          frame arriving. Both read as a flash of the wrong colour. Setting it
          to the same white as everything else closes them.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/zigly_splash_ground</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The launch theme. This is what the window wears from the moment the
      launcher icon is tapped until React Native's first frame.

      WHY IT EXISTS. See ../drawable/zigly_splash.xml for the full reasoning:
      in short, every other loading cover in this app is a React component, and
      none of them are on screen during the ~1-3s a mid-range Android spends
      booting the JS bundle. The WebView mounts and starts painting zigly.com in
      that window, so without a launch theme the first thing the customer sees
      is the raw mobile website. This is the only layer that can cover it,
      because it is the only one that does not need JavaScript to be running.

      MainActivity swaps back to AppTheme in onCreate — see MainActivity.kt.
      That handoff is what stops this drawable staying on screen behind the app.
    -->
    <style name="AppLaunchTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <!--
          Pre-Android-12 (including the Samsung A31 this was reported on): the
          window background IS the splash, drawn by the window manager before
          any of the app's code runs.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/zigly_splash</item>

        <!--
          Android 12+ ignores windowBackground for the launch and draws the
          platform splash screen instead, so the same intent has to be stated
          again in its own vocabulary. Without these three the app would launch
          to a bare launcher icon on the system's colour on new devices while
          being correct on old ones.

          These attributes are only read on API 31+, and naming them here is
          safe on older platforms: unknown attributes in a theme are ignored,
          which is why this does not need a values-v31 variant.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/zigly_splash_ground</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@mipmap/zigly_splash_logo</item>
        <!--
          Hold the platform splash for a beat rather than letting it snap away
          the instant the activity is ready. 0 would let Android 12+ tear it
          down before the JS splash exists, reopening the very gap this closes.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration">400</item>
    </style>

</resources>
